 with the aforementioned stealth  mechanics extends XCOM 2's longevity  long past that of enemy Unknown in which  missions grew boring over time in fact  the entirety of XCOM to unfold in  original ways each time you play it's a  multi-layered experience in which nuance  and refinement exists on each level but  they also combine to form an amorphous  ever-changing whole the squad firefights  lay the foundation with an array of  tactical problems to solve  there are terrain considerations from  the elevation of the battlefield to the  sight lines of the map structures then  there are character classes  a world of possibilities to consider  from the sharpshooter's range to  specialists hacking abilities and more  often than not there's a countdown timer  pushing you forward numbering the turns  until your target escapes or the aliens  extract their secret data there's always  something to worry about something to  consider some way things could go  completely wrong as you fight to keep  your squad alive  XCOM 2's overarching strategy layer  imparts the same sense of urgency and a  similar bevy of obstacles as we move the  humans mobile avenger base across the  globe establishing links with other  rebel cells we also fight the aliens  plan to erase humanity indicated by a  crimson timer at the top of a world map  a real sense of tension forces us to  maintain a constant state of motion  whether by freeing a waylaid country  researching new alien weaponry or  rushing production on a crucial new  structure and yet underlying all of this  is a more personal current we grow  attached to individual soldiers as they  perform spectacular feats and rise in  the ranks we rely on our best leaders  and root for rookies to prove themselves  XCOM to maybe a war game but it doesn't  ignore the boots on the ground strategy  plans after all are only as good as the  soldiers putting them into action in the  end X come to is fantastic not just  because of its refined individual layers  